LS(1)

NAME
     ls - list files

SYNOPSIS
     ls [-ladtr] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
     Lists what is in a directory: the one you are in, or the ones you
     name.

     -l     long form: one file per line, with details (see below)
     -a     include files whose names begin with a dot
     -d     describe a directory itself, not what is inside it
     -t     newest first
     -r     reverse the order

     A line of the long form reads, from left to right:

         -rw-r--r--  1 root  wheel  5120 Aug 29 16:40 ledger

     the kind of file and its permissions; a link count; the owner; the
     group; the size in characters; when it was last changed; its name.

     The first character is d for a directory and - for a file. The
     nine after it are three sets of rwx (read, write, run): for the
     owner, for the group, and for everyone else.

SEE ALSO
     chmod(1), cd(1)
